<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oromo Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as OLF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> Liberation Front is a political organisation founded in 1973 to pursue self-determination for the Oromo people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It joined the Transitional Government formed after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> in 1991 but withdrew in 1992, accusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> of persecution, and resumed armed struggle. Its leaders returned to Ethiopia from exile in 2018, though an armed wing continued fighting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oromo-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Oromo Liberation Front" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Ollé, see es:Wikipedia:Autorizaciones/Banderas de Jaume Ollé · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Oromo_Liberation_Front.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> lost their independence to Abyssinia in the last quarter of the nineteenth century, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> the Oromo language was banned from schools and government. In 1967 his regime outlawed the Mecha and Tuluma Self-Help Association, arresting and killing many of its members and arresting its leader, the officer Tadesse Birru. One member, Hussein Sora, escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and with other refugees formed the Ethiopian National Liberation Front. The OLF itself was founded at a secret conference attended by Hussein Sora and Elemo Qiltu, which adopted the armed Oromo units of the Chercher Mountains as its military wing, the Oromo Liberation Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>That first army was destroyed by mortar fire at the Battle of Tiro on 6 September 1974, where Elemo Qiltu was killed. A Founding Congress in 1976 issued a new programme calling for the total liberation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> nation from Ethiopian colonialism, and the front spread into western Oromia, taking land in Wellega. In 1990 the Tigrayan People's Liberation Front gathered new ethnic parties under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front</a>, whose Oromo People's Democratic Organization was seen as an attempt to undermine the OLF.</p><p>After the Eritrean–Ethiopian War much of the leadership moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. In 2008 Brigadier General Kemel Gelchu announced that the OLF would lay down its arms, and nearly half its southern army surrendered before he was removed. The main faction and a breakaway group reunited on 20 November 2012. After the peace agreement of August 2018 the Oromo Liberation Army split from the OLF over disarmament and kept fighting; on 11 August 2021 its leader Kumsa Diriba announced an alliance with the Tigrayan People's Liberation Front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The OLF has offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, broadcasts by radio in Afaan Oromo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>, and is a member of the Unrepresented Nations and Peoples Organization. Its members have not agreed whether self-determination should take the form of an independent Oromia or a place in a democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Critics such as Merera Gudina dispute its term Abyssinian colonialism, pointing to the Yejju Oromo who controlled the imperial seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gonder</a> for about eighty years in the 1700s; the OLF and many Oromo intellectuals answer that such criticism is propaganda meant to delegitimise the movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oromo Liberation Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Oromo Liberation Front</b> initially joined the government but withdrew in 1992.</blockquote><a href="/state/transitional-government-of-ethiopia">Transitional Government of Ethiopia</a></li></ul></section>
